msgstr ""

#: includes/class-pncp-admin.php:859
msgid "CDN shared secret (optional):"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-pncp-admin.php:859
msgid "Only when \"Real visitor IP\" is a CDN header (CF-Connecting-IP, X-Real-IP, X-Forwarded-For). A CDN (Bunny, Cloudflare) is multi-tenant: its edge IPs are shared, so by default anyone could point their own CDN zone at your origin and set the real-IP header. Set a secret here and configure your CDN to send it on every request as the header “X-ScrapeLock-CDN”; the CDN’s real-IP header is then trusted only when the secret matches. Leave empty to keep the current behaviour. For best effect, also restrict your origin so it is reachable only through the CDN."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-pncp-admin.php:860
msgid "Only through the CDN: refuse (403) requests that do not bring the secret"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-pncp-admin.php:860
msgid "Someone who finds your server's own address can go around the CDN and everything it blocks. With this on, every request without the secret in the X-ScrapeLock-CDN header gets a 403, also pages from the page cache when the pre-cache check is on. First make the CDN add the header to every request: in Bunny CDN, an Edge Rule with the action “Set Request Header” (X-ScrapeLock-CDN = your secret) for all requests; in Cloudflare, Rules → Transform Rules → “Modify Request Header”, set static. It can only be turned on from a request that brings the secret, so you cannot lock yourself out by mistake. Not affected: the server itself (cron, cache preload), WP-CLI. Files the web server sends without WordPress (images, CSS, JS) are not covered. If the CDN ever stops sending the header, add define( 'SCRAPELOCK_CDN_ONLY', false ); to wp-config.php."
msgstr ""

